1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,428
What does a person like that actually want to hear?

2
00:00:03,428 --> 00:00:06,505
Priya's conversation with Marcus took four minutes.

3
00:00:06,505 --> 00:00:11,213
Not because he was easy to convince, but because she had already done the work

4
00:00:11,213 --> 00:00:16,282
Section 9 walked you through: a real number, honestly counted, and a clear answer to

5
00:00:16,282 --> 00:00:17,489
what could go wrong.

6
00:00:17,489 --> 00:00:21,472
You have that same work sitting in your own C09 tracker right now.

7
00:00:21,472 --> 00:00:26,360
Remember who Marcus is, from the day he first appeared in this course: a director

8
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:31,127
who wants numbers, not enthusiasm, and who is sceptical of "AI" as a word while

9
00:00:31,127 --> 00:00:34,688
being entirely persuadable by a saved afternoon he can see.

10
00:00:34,688 --> 00:00:39,455
That is not one man's quirk, it is the default setting of everyone who approves

11
00:00:39,455 --> 00:00:40,662
things for a living.

12
00:00:40,662 --> 00:00:45,611
They have sat through vendor demos that went nowhere and pilots that quietly died,

13
00:00:45,611 --> 00:00:48,206
and the word AI now arrives pre-discounted.

14
00:00:48,206 --> 00:00:53,215
Which is good news for you, because the discount applies to claims, and you are not

15
00:00:53,215 --> 00:00:54,180
bringing claims.

16
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:56,594
You are bringing six weeks of a tracker.

17
00:00:57,144 --> 00:01:01,818
The second version works because it leads with the thing a careful manager is

18
00:01:01,818 --> 00:01:06,856
actually listening for: evidence that you have already considered the downside, not

19
00:01:06,856 --> 00:01:07,827
just the upside.

20
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:12,866
Yes, "what nearly went wrong" means telling Marcus about the near miss, and telling

21
00:01:12,866 --> 00:01:13,715
it on purpose.

22
00:01:13,715 --> 00:01:18,268
The instinct to hide Section 8's story from the pitch is exactly backwards.

23
00:01:18,268 --> 00:01:22,820
Told properly, in one breath, it is the strongest slide you have: a mistake

24
00:01:22,820 --> 00:01:27,919
happened, the guardrail held, nothing reached a client, and the process got stricter

25
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:28,587
afterwards.

26
00:01:28,587 --> 00:01:33,382
That is not a confession, it is a demonstration that your safety story has been

27
00:01:33,382 --> 00:01:36,417
tested by reality rather than asserted on a slide.

28
00:01:36,417 --> 00:01:41,395
Managers do not expect zero incidents, they expect contained ones, and you are one

29
00:01:41,395 --> 00:01:45,462
of the few people who can prove containment rather than promise it.

30
00:01:45,462 --> 00:01:50,075
Tell the story in three sentences, unprompted, and watch what it does to the

31
00:01:50,075 --> 00:01:51,532
temperature of the room.

32
00:01:52,082 --> 00:01:57,141
If you cannot answer this question about your own agents yet, that is worth noticing

33
00:01:57,141 --> 00:01:58,587
before you pitch anyone.

34
00:01:58,587 --> 00:02:03,586
Go back to Section 1's job description and Section 8's worksheet until you can, and

35
00:02:03,586 --> 00:02:05,935
only then book the actual conversation.

36
00:02:05,935 --> 00:02:10,211
And make the ask genuinely specific, because vague asks are where these

37
00:02:10,211 --> 00:02:11,657
conversations go to die.

38
00:02:11,657 --> 00:02:16,475
"Support for using AI more" gives a manager nothing to approve and everything to

39
00:02:16,475 --> 00:02:17,198
worry about.

40
00:02:17,198 --> 00:02:22,136
Priya's ask was one sentence: permission to share four workflow templates with the

41
00:02:22,136 --> 00:02:27,015
three others on her team, each person connecting their own accounts, each running

42
00:02:27,015 --> 00:02:28,400
the same trial she ran.

43
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,315
Small, bounded, reversible, and costing Marcus nothing but a yes.

44
00:02:32,315 --> 00:02:36,832
A manager who cannot approve that in the meeting was never going to approve

45
00:02:36,832 --> 00:02:39,543
anything, and now you know that too, cheaply.

46
00:02:40,093 --> 00:02:45,159
Practise the four parts aloud once before any real meeting, in order, without notes.

47
00:02:45,159 --> 00:02:49,863
Not to memorise a script, but because the order carries the persuasion: built,

48
00:02:49,863 --> 00:02:51,492
saved, safeguarded, asking.

49
00:02:51,492 --> 00:02:56,136
If you find yourself starting with the ask, or worse with the technology, the

50
00:02:56,136 --> 00:03:01,081
structure has collapsed into the vendor pitch your listener has already survived a

51
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:01,805
dozen times.

52
00:03:01,805 --> 00:03:06,811
Ninety seconds of rehearsal keeps the spine straight under questions, and questions

53
00:03:06,811 --> 00:03:11,696
are a good sign: a manager probing your safeguards is a manager already imagining

54
00:03:11,696 --> 00:03:12,359
saying yes.

55
00:03:12,359 --> 00:03:15,797
The pitch that fails is rarely the one that gets grilled.

56
00:03:15,797 --> 00:03:20,682
It is the one that gets a polite nod and no follow-up, because nothing in it gave

57
00:03:20,682 --> 00:03:23,758
the listener anything solid enough to push against.

58
00:03:23,758 --> 00:03:28,644
Next lecture turns this structure into the actual one-pager you hand over, so you

59
00:03:28,644 --> 00:03:33,469
are not reconstructing it from memory in the meeting, on the spot, with everyone

60
00:03:33,469 --> 00:03:34,011
watching.
